# On top of 0126: TF_GLM_PRX's failures end in bounded time, and an idle proxy sleeps. No new switch to turn on, no
# change to a bit of any reply (four Sparks cabled as a ring, 2026-10-21: five solo greedy prompts of 5K-13K tokens,
# identical replies with and without).
#
# Failures (cuda/prx_proxy.c): besides a failed completion, a proxy fails when nothing advances for
#   TF_GLM_PRX_TIMEOUT_S (300) while calls are outstanding, when a neighbour's heartbeat (a counter every proxy writes
#   to both neighbours every 0.5 s, busy or idle; a dead neighbour also fails that write after the queue pair's
#   retries) stands still for TF_GLM_PRX_PEER_S (10) while calls are outstanding, when a neighbour's abort arrives, or
#   when the rank's other channel failed. It then writes its abort to both neighbours (the failure goes round the
#   ring), moves its queue pairs to the error state and holds every word the GPU can wait on (flags, send_done,
#   consumed) at posted + 2^30 (CU_STREAM_WAIT_VALUE_GEQ compares cyclically) until prx_destroy: no GPU wait outlives
#   a failure. prx_post then refuses calls, so the next call raises, and the samplers (multi.sample_streams,
#   multi_tune.sample_packed, decode.sample_rows) raise once they read a sample (comm.check_prx), before a token
#   computed after a released wait reaches a client; a failed iteration names the exchange's failure rather than what
#   the GPU did on the released data (a CUDA error). TF_GLM_PRX_EXIT_S (10) after the failure the process exits
#   (code 70), so start.sh's FOREGROUND watcher (or a router) restarts the server (0: it stays up, broken, as before).
#   TF_GLM_PRX_FAULT=R:N (a test): on rank R, call N goes out with a bad rkey. /health shows each channel's calls,
#   longest wait with calls outstanding, sleeps and failure (prx, prx_share).
# Idle: a proxy caught up with every posted call (nothing outstanding: nothing can happen before the next prx_post)
#   sleeps on a futex after TF_GLM_PRX_IDLE_MS (20); prx_post and prx_destroy wake it, and it wakes each second for
#   its heartbeat. An idle server's two proxy threads a Spark no longer hold a core each (0: they poll, as in 0126).

